<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bujangga Manik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bujangga Manik is an Old Sundanese poem of about 1,600 lines, preserved in a single palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> since 1627. It tells of a Sundanese prince of the Pakuan court who became a hermit and travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, and its many place names make it a key source for the geography of the Sunda Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bujangga-manik.jpg" alt="Bujangga Manik" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bujangga Manik · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bujangga_Manik.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
